# Source-distribution contents.
#
# TWO JOBS. (1) The shipped benchmark rows and their manifests must be IN the sdist and
# the wheel -- a benchmark whose row files are missing is not reproducible, and a
# frozen row manifest is what makes a changed upstream fail loudly instead of silently
# redefining the benchmark. (2) NOTHING from the sibling `internal/` tree may ever be
# packaged: it holds unreleased data, controlled-access sources, run outputs and the
# licensing working notes. `internal/` sits outside this sdist root, so it cannot be
# reached by a relative path, but the prune lines below are kept as a second wall and
# CI asserts the property on the built artifacts (`packaging` job in ci.yml).

# EXPECTED BUILD NOISE. `python -m build` prints "warning: no files found matching ..."
# for the patterns below that match nothing in a given checkout (a root `NOTICE`, or the
# in-package `epigauge/benchmarks/` location while the rows live at the root). The
# patterns are kept deliberately so neither location can be dropped by accident; CI
# asserts on the BUILT artifacts, which is the check that actually binds.

# --- Legal and provenance -------------------------------------------------------
include LICENSE
include NOTICE
include README.md
include CONTRIBUTING.md
include pyproject.toml

# --- Benchmark rows, manifests and their per-directory notices ------------------
# Both locations are covered on purpose. `epigauge/benchmarks/` is inside the import
# package and therefore also lands in the WHEEL (via [tool.setuptools.package-data]);
# a top-level `benchmarks/` tree is sdist-only, because a wheel cannot carry data
# outside the package directory. The ITSNdb slice keeps its own LICENSE/NOTICE beside
# its rows -- those files are part of the data, not decoration, so they are grafted in
# explicitly and must never be pruned.
recursive-include epigauge/benchmarks *.jsonl *.json *.csv *.tsv *.md
recursive-include epigauge/benchmarks LICENSE* NOTICE* README*
recursive-include benchmarks *.jsonl *.json *.csv *.tsv *.md
recursive-include benchmarks LICENSE* NOTICE* README*

# --- Reproduction surface -------------------------------------------------------
# The fixtures ship: `python3 -I epigauge/tests/test_*.py` must work from an unpacked
# sdist, for a reviewer who has no pytest.
recursive-include epigauge/tests *.py
recursive-include scripts *.py
include .github/workflows/ci.yml

# --- Supplementary data for the preprint ----------------------------------------
# The paper's supplementary index cites `supplementary/artifacts/...` and
# `supplementary/scripts/...`, so both must be IN the sdist: a cited path that is not
# distributed is the defect this directory exists to fix. Only the five scripts that run
# against data this package ships are here; the annotation scripts need a 1.35 GB IEDB
# export that is not redistributable, so their artifacts ship and their code does not.
# `supplementary/proteomes/` is deliberately NOT packaged -- the nine UniProt FASTA files
# are third-party downloads under their own terms, and the two scripts that need them
# fail loudly and name them rather than falling back to a weaker clustering basis.
recursive-include supplementary *.json *.py *.md

# --- Never packaged -------------------------------------------------------------
# internal/ in every form it could be referenced under, belt and braces.
prune internal
prune ../internal
prune */internal
global-exclude internal/*
recursive-exclude internal *

# Working trees, caches and OS litter.
prune .git
prune .github/scratch
prune dist
prune build
global-exclude *.py[cod] __pycache__/* .DS_Store *.so *.swp *~
global-exclude .env .env.* *.pem *.key credentials.json secrets.yaml

# Large upstream corpora are fetched, not shipped. Only the derived, licence-cleared
# rows above are redistributed.
prune data
prune outputs

# The README gif, so sdist readers see the same page as GitHub.
recursive-include assets *.gif
